\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity, yet no prior work causally links ambiguity to safety violations. Using a matched-pair design on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type taxonomy evaluated across five models, we show that ambiguous clauses increase violations by $+$34.0 percentage points overall and $+$31.0\,pp for the five types whose manipulation is strictly disambiguating (incompleteness conflates ambiguity with information addition); cross-benchmark evaluation on SOPBench shows directional consistency ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean; all five $\Delta > 0$), and a healthcare pilot extends the effect beyond customer service ($+$58.8\,pp). Error analysis reveals that agents almost never recognize ambiguity and converge on identical violations with strong permissive bias, showing that agreement-based safety monitoring is blind to systematic policy misinterpretation. These findings motivate clause-level policy auditing over type-level risk screening.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity---a pilot audit of 407 clauses from 24 production policy documents finds a majority of clauses contain at least one ambiguity type (55--87\%, depending on union vs.\ intersection of two judges; $\kappa = 0.30$; Appendix~\ref{app:prevalence})---a property studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared context ensures convergent interpretation. We document a distinct failure mode that breaks this assumption: LLM agents \emph{converge} on the same incorrect interpretation, making ambiguity harmful through systematic, undetectable error rather than inconsistency (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). We term this \emph{convergent nocuity}: Berry's criterion---predicated on divergent interpretations---does not extend to LLM agents, where convergence reflects shared training biases rather than shared domain knowledge, and errors are invisible to agreement-based monitoring.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. (a)~Six-type ambiguity taxonomy (two layers) with 300 matched clause pairs. (b)~Matched-pair causal design: each clause pair is injected into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents; five models interact under both ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across 2,997 episodes. (c)~Convergent nocuity: models independently converge on the same permissive interpretation, producing unanimous agreement that passes consensus-based safety monitors---a systematic blind spot invisible to multi-agent checks.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy})---we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal a \emph{type--mechanism dissociation}: policy ambiguity consistently increases violations ($+34.0$\,pp under synthetic conditions; $+31.0$\,pp excluding incompleteness), but ambiguity \emph{type} predicts \emph{how} agents fail, not \emph{whether} they fail. Clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance while type membership explains only 0.4\%; yet type predicts failure mechanism substantially more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$ on tables of different dimensionality; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}). This dissociation suggests the taxonomy may function as a diagnostic instrument rather than a risk classifier (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Incompleteness is the highest-risk type (46--62\%); the remaining types form a partial ordering with robust extremes but margin-dependent mid-tier structure (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}).

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study establishing that policy ambiguity increases violations by $+$34.0\,pp ($+$31.0\,pp excluding incompleteness; $+$18.7\,pp for the confound-minimized coreferential type), with convergent evidence on SOPBench ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean; all five $\Delta > 0$), with clause wording---not type membership---as the primary risk factor among measured predictors (22.4\% vs.\ 0.4\% of variance; \S\ref{sec:results}); and (2)~\emph{convergent nocuity}---a failure mode in which 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations across architecturally diverse models (Jaccard $= 0.46$ cross-family vs.\ $0.54$ within-family), with 77\% permissive bias, rendering agreement-based monitoring blind to systematic failures (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). An exploratory type--mechanism dissociation is reported in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch). Appendix~\ref{app:pipeline} details the construction pipeline; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_examples} provides representative examples for each type.

Clause pairs are hand-crafted following structured type-level specifications and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% initial agreement (287/300; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.95$ on six-type classification); this high agreement reflects annotation precision---each pair differs only in the ambiguity-introducing edit---not task simplicity. The 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models from four providers spanning two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares conditions within the same model--judge pairing, systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); cross-model judge asymmetry affects only absolute rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify episodes on a four-tier severity scale (none, minor, moderate, critical); our primary threshold is moderate-or-above (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes yields $\kappa = 0.653$ ($\kappa_w = 0.775$; per-type $n \approx 17$, CIs wide; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}). Three-family validation (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}) yields Fleiss' $\kappa = 0.717$. The judge prompt template is in Appendix~\ref{app:judge_prompt}.

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two annotators, blind to LLM labels, labeled 100 stratified episodes. Inter-annotator $\kappa = 0.798$; human--LLM $\kappa = 0.733$ ($\kappa_w = 0.807$), approaching inter-annotator levels and validating the automated pipeline \citep{zheng_llm_judge_2024}. Per-type estimates ($n \approx 32$--$34$) have wide CIs (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}).

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Within-family replicability.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates yields ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$); cross-provider concordance is moderate (Kendall's $W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; \S\ref{sec:results}).

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary SESOI of $\pm 15$ pp. We report the matched-pair odds ratio from McNemar's test on discordant outcomes ($n_{\text{disc}} = 580$ of 1{,}497 pairs), which conditions on within-pair disagreement and thus differs from the crude marginal odds ratio. No industry-standard SESOI exists for LLM agent policy violations. We use $\pm 15$\,pp as a pragmatic threshold: it exceeds measurement noise (perturbation control upper CI: 6.67\,pp) by $>$2$\times$, and a per-type difference below this margin would not warrant distinct mitigation strategies. This is a pragmatic default, not a principled domain-anchored threshold (see Appendix~\ref{app:tost} for cost anchoring); Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports sensitivity across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$\,pp; \citealp{anvari_lakens_2021}). The margin-free bootstrap cluster analysis (\S\ref{sec:results}; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirms the same equivalence structure. We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction within three pre-registered test families (Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparison}); post-hoc power exceeds 0.99 for C1 and C2.

