\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes ($\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels. Type--mechanism mutual information (NMI $= 0.23$; $V = 0.45$, 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against LLM-coding circularity.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary; human-coded $V = 0.40$ (mean of two annotators; collapsed $V = 0.42$; Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}) corroborates the LLM-coded association ($V = 0.45$), which remains a moderate upper bound. The higher LLM agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$) than human ($0.32$) may reflect shared classification conventions---a meta-level parallel to convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity})---so these patterns are best interpreted as categorization tendencies.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

Three failure patterns emerge with $\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency (Figure~\ref{fig:type_mechanism}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure_details}): \emph{structural misparse} dominates scopal, conditional precedence, and coreferential types; \emph{information gap-filling} dominates incompleteness and lexical; and \emph{boundary violation} dominates authorization scope exclusively (69.2\%, $p < 10^{-4}$).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\%, with GPT-4.1 and Claude at 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational level to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Convergent Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors---whereas divergent nocuity produces detectable disagreement, convergent nocuity renders errors invisible to agreement-based monitoring. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{convergent nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Under an independence assumption, the expected $\geq$3/5 convergence rate is 32.4\%; the observed 41.3\% exceeds this by $\sim$9\,pp ($p < 0.0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_family}). These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}). Cross-family Jaccard ($0.46$; Figure~\ref{fig:convergent_nocuity}) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$; $\Delta = 0.084$ [0.016, 0.153]), indicating shared architectural biases rather than training-data overlap. The ambient convergent violation rate under unambiguous conditions is 1.7\% (5/300 clauses), confirming a $+$39.6\,pp ambiguity-induced excess. Excluding incompleteness, the excess over the independence baseline remains robust ($+$8.3\,pp, 95\% CI $[2.2, 14.3]$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:nocuity_sans_incomp}).

\section{TOST Equivalence Sensitivity}
\label{app:tost}

Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports the TOST equivalence analysis \citep{lakens_2017} across seven margins. Three pairs involving incompleteness never reach equivalence even at $\pm 20$ pp: conditional precedence--incompleteness ($\Delta = 16.8$ pp), coreferential--incompleteness ($\Delta = 22.0$ pp), and incompleteness--scopal ($\Delta = 14.2$ pp).

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{TOST equivalence sensitivity across margins. Mid-tier = fraction of the six within-cluster pairs (lexical, auth.\ scope, scopal, cond.\ prec.) that are equivalent. Inc.\ and Cor.\ columns indicate separation from the mid-tier cluster.}
\label{tab:tost_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Margin & Equiv.\ pairs & Mid-tier & Inc.\ sep. & Cor.\ sep. \\
\midrule
$\pm 5$ pp & 0/15 & 0/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 8$ pp & 0/15 & 0/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 10$ pp & 3/15 & 3/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 12$ pp & 5/15 & 5/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 15$ pp & 8/15 & 6/6 & \checkmark & -- \\
$\pm 18$ pp & 9/15 & 6/6 & \checkmark & -- \\
$\pm 20$ pp & 12/15 & 6/6 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cost anchoring.} Under a per-incident cost assumption $c$ per violation, a 15\,pp threshold over $N$ deployment episodes implies a cost differential of $0.15 \times N \times c$. For moderate-scale deployments ($N = 10{,}000$ episodes) with per-violation costs ranging from \$10 (minor policy deviation) to \$500 (regulatory exposure), the threshold maps to \$15{,}000--\$750{,}000 in differential risk---well above typical audit and remediation costs, supporting the practical relevance of the observed effects.

\section{Mitigation Experiment}
\label{app:mitigation}

Table~\ref{tab:mitigation} reports the per-type, per-model $\Delta$pp from the disambiguation-instruction mitigation experiment (1,256 treatment episodes across five of six types; coreferential was not tested). Coreferential pairs are excluded from both the mitigation and replication experiments. In mitigation, their ambiguous and unambiguous variants differ only in pronoun resolution, making it impossible to craft targeted disambiguation instructions without revealing the experimental manipulation. In replication, the $\tau^2$-bench episode design for coreferential ambiguity is tightly coupled to the original five-model configuration; extending it to new model families would require redesigning the episode templates, which falls outside the scope of this confirmatory replication. The treatment appends a type-specific disambiguation instruction to the agent's system prompt. Control rates are the matched 5-type subset from the full study (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\paragraph{Prompt text.} All five types use the same template, substituting only the type name: ``{[}DISAMBIGUATION NOTICE{]} This policy clause may contain \emph{[type]} ambiguity. Before executing any action related to this clause: (1) Identify what is ambiguous or underspecified in the policy; (2) If clarification is available from the user/system, request it; (3) If no clarification channel exists, adopt the most conservative interpretation; (4) Log your interpretation explicitly before proceeding.'' The generic awareness prompt (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) omits the type label: ``The policy may contain ambiguous clauses. If unclear, ask for clarification before acting.''

\begin{table*}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Violation rate change ($\Delta$pp) after adding type-specific disambiguation instructions. Negative values indicate reduction. Baseline violation rates are shown in parentheses. Coreferential was excluded because it was not included in the mitigation experiment.}
\label{tab:mitigation}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
Type & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & Pooled \\
\midrule
Auth.\ scope & $-40.0$ (64) & $-26.0$ (46) & $0.0$ (12) & $-14.0$ (26) & $-10.0$ (60) & $-18.0$ (42) \\
Lexical & $-16.5$ (42) & $-10.0$ (34) & $-8.0$ (32) & $-8.0$ (52) & $-2.0$ (54) & $-8.9$ (43) \\
Cond.\ prec. & $-4.6$ (34) & $-10.0$ (36) & $-12.0$ (36) & $-2.0$ (40) & $-12.0$ (38) & $-8.1$ (37) \\
Scopal & $-6.7$ (42) & $-4.0$ (38) & $-10.0$ (32) & $-2.7$ (37) & $+2.0$ (48) & $-4.3$ (39) \\
Incomp. & $-2.6$ (46) & $-14.0$ (58) & $-2.0$ (48) & $-2.0$ (62) & $+4.0$ (54) & $-3.4$ (54) \\
\midrule
Overall & $-14.0$ (46) & $-12.8$ (42) & $-6.4$ (32) & $-5.8$ (43) & $-3.6$ (51) & $-8.5$ (43) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\paragraph{Generic vs.\ type-specific prompts.}
We additionally tested a generic disambiguation prompt (``Before acting, check whether the policy clause could be interpreted in more than one way; if so, ask the customer for clarification'') against the type-specific prompts reported above, using GPT-5.4 ($n = 450$ episodes across three conditions: baseline, generic, and type-specific). Table~\ref{tab:generic_vs_specific} reports the results. The generic prompt produced no significant reduction in any type (overall $\Delta = +1.3$\,pp, all per-type McNemar $p > 0.05$), consistent with the type-dependent effectiveness pattern: authorization scope showed the largest (non-significant) reduction under both generic ($-$6.0\,pp) and type-specific ($-$12.0\,pp) prompts, while incompleteness showed a non-significant \emph{increase} under the type-specific prompt ($+$14.0\,pp, $p = 0.118$). This single-model pilot has limited statistical power ($n = 50$ per cell); multi-model validation is left to future work. The pattern nevertheless reinforces the main finding that prompt-level intervention is insufficient for specification-layer ambiguity, particularly incompleteness, where the missing information cannot be recovered from the prompt alone.

